So it seems there's a mix between Local and Domain groups; if we use the powershell command `Get-LocalGroup | Select *` when can see that the SIDs correspond to the ones shown in the `groups` table.

In `processes` and `users` we use the `NetUserGetInfo` API to retrieve the `uid` and `gid`, and specifically we take the `gid` from the `usri3_primary_group_id`, but that is a Domain group, while instead I believe we should use `NetUserGetLocalGroups`. Running the MSDN example on my user returns the correct Local group name.

The `groups` table shows the Local groups correctly because it uses the `NetLocalGroupEnum` API.


@Smjert I took a look at this issue. And still it seems to be present on the current master.
Based the information so far in the issue. I noticed that most of the places, where we use the api to fetch information for SID. We seemed to passing nullptr as server name, which implies local groups only.

After various tries. Here is what i found that could possibly fix this.

**Problem:**
We use **TOKEN_USER** which returns User SID here. 
https://github.com/osquery/osquery/blob/master/osquery/tables/system/windows/processes.cpp#L390

But later on we interpret this as **PTOKEN_OWNER** (even though internally both structures are same and contain PSID)
https://github.com/osquery/osquery/blob/master/osquery/tables/system/windows/processes.cpp#L416

But when querying token information based on this information class **GetTokenInformation** will return different SID.
https://github.com/osquery/osquery/blob/master/osquery/tables/system/windows/processes.cpp#L396

Ideally we should use **TOKEN_USER** to create **UID** and **TOKEN_OWNER** for **GID**.

I ended up printing the various groups(via **TOKEN_GROUPS** as well) the user belonged to initially and eventually realized that **TOKEN_OWNER** itself should suffice for our case to determine **GID**.


